[category {Map Display and Supporting Utilities}][keywords map]
[keywords {file, geo/point}]
[keywords {geo/point, file}]
[moddesc   {Map display support}]
[titledesc {Reading/writing tklib geo/point files}]
[require Tcl 8.6-]
[require debug]
[require debug::caller]
[require map::slippy 0.8]
[require snit]
[require map::point::file [opt [vset VERSION]]]
[description]

This package provides commands to read and write Tklib [term geo/point] files. Unlike the files for
areas, boxes and tracks, a single [term geo/point] file can hold any number of points. The format
of these files is described in section [sectref {File format}], the value of a single point in
section [sectref {geo/point value}].

[para] Packages using these files are [package map::point::store::fs], which loads all
[term geo/point] files of a directory, and through it [package map::point::store::memory].

[section API]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd {::map point file read}] [arg path]]

Reads the [term geo/point] file [arg path] and returns its contents as a list of
[term geo/point] values, one per point in the file, in the order of the file. See section
[sectref {geo/point value}] for the format of the elements.

[para] If the file cannot be opened, or is not a valid [term geo/point] file, the result is the
empty string, i.e. an empty list. No error is thrown in these cases. For an invalid file a short
message describing the problem is written to [const stdout]. A file containing only the magic line
is valid, and contains no points.

[para] A point which does not specify any name gets a single name, built from the name of the file
without directory and extension, a slash, and a sequence number counting the unnamed points of the
file, starting at [const 1]. For example, the second unnamed point in file
[file /data/cafes.points] is named [const cafes/2].

[para] A point which does not specify a kind gets the kind [const point].

[call [cmd {::map point file write}] [arg path] [arg points]]

Writes the list of [term geo/point] values [arg points] (see section [sectref {geo/point value}])
to the file [arg path], replacing any previous contents. Identical points are written only once,
and the order of the points in the file may differ from their order in the list. For each point
the kind (if not empty) is written first, followed by the names in dictionary order, with duplicates
removed, followed by the coordinates. The coordinates are not validated.

[para] The result of the command is the empty string. Failure to open the file is reported as an
error.

[list_end]

[section {geo/point value}]

A [term geo/point] is a single location on the surface of Earth, together with a kind and a list of
names. It is represented by a Tcl dictionary with the following keys:

[list_begin definitions]
[def [const names]]
A list of strings, the names of the point. Several names can be specified, e.g. aliases or names in
different languages.

[def [const kind]]
A string classifying the point. [package map::point::map-display] uses the kind as the name of the
style to show the point with. The kinds known to it by default are [const point],
[const feature], and [const cluster]. The kind [const cluster] is used by
[package map::point::store::memory] for the clusters of points it creates, and should not be used
for ordinary points.

[def [const geo]]
The location of the point, a list of two numbers, the latitude and the longitude in degrees, in this
order (the [term geo] of [package map::slippy]).
[list_end]

For example:

[example {names {{Prospect Point} Lookout} geo {49.3134 -123.1416} kind feature}]

[section {File format}]

A [term geo/point] file is a text file, read line by line:

[list_begin itemized]
[item] Leading and trailing whitespace of each line is ignored.

[item] Empty lines are ignored.

[item] Lines starting with [const #] or [const //] are comments, and ignored.

[item] The first line which is neither empty nor a comment must be the magic line
[const tklib/geo/point], identifying the file.

[item] The remaining lines describe the points, one after the other. Each point is described by
zero or more lines with its kind and names, followed by exactly two lines with the latitude and the
longitude of its location, in this order.

[item] A line which is a number is a coordinate. The latitude must be in the range -90 to 90, the
longitude in the range -180 to 180 degrees. After the second coordinate of a point the next line
starts a new point.

[item] A line starting with [const kind:] specifies the kind of the point. The kind is the rest of
the line, without leading and trailing whitespace. If several such lines are given for a point, the
last one is used.

[item] Every other line is a name of the point. A name cannot look like a floating point number, it
would be read as a coordinate, and cannot start with [const kind:].

[item] The file must not end with an incomplete point, i.e. with names or kind without coordinates,
or with a single coordinate.

[list_end]

[para] Files conventionally use the extension [file .points]. This is also the default file pattern
of [package map::point::store::fs].

[para] For example, a file with two points, the second of them without a name:

[example {tklib/geo/point

kind: feature
Prospect Point
Lookout
49.3134
-123.1416

# kind "point", named after the file
49.3000
-123.1300}]

[section EXAMPLE]

[example_begin]
package require map::point::file

set f [lb]open sights.points w[rb]
puts $f "tklib/geo/point"
puts $f "kind:feature\nProspect Point\n49.3134\n-123.1416"
puts $f "Siwash Rock\n49.3105\n-123.1530"
close $f

foreach point [lb]map point file read sights.points[rb] {
    dict with point {
        puts "[lb]join $names /[rb] ($kind) at $geo"
    }
}
[example_end]

